    "fullContent": "The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has unveiled comprehensive regulatory updates governing the functioning of Asset Reconstruction Companies (ARCs) operating within the country. Aimed at cleaning up financial balance sheets and expediting the resolution of stressed assets, the revised framework raises the minimum Net Owned Fund (NOF) requirement for ARCs to Rs 300 crore, ensuring that only financially robust entities participate in the distress debt market. Furthermore, the central bank has introduced stringent governance mandates, requiring a higher proportion of independent directors on ARC boards and establishing specialized committees to oversee asset acquisition and valuation processes. ARCs will now be subjected to more frequent audits and mandated public disclosures regarding the recovery timelines and realization values of acquired non-performing assets (NPAs). Banking and finance exam aspirants should closely track these regulatory updates, as questions regarding RBI guidelines, NPA management, and the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code (IBC) frequently feature in RBI Grade B, IBPS PO, and UPSC exams. These reforms are crucial for maintaining systemic financial stability and enhancing investor confidence in India's banking sector.",
