    "fullContent": "The **Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO)** has achieved a major technological milestone by successfully testing the high-thrust semi-cryogenic engine intended for the **Next-Gen Launch Vehicle (NGLV)**. The test, conducted at the ISRO Propulsion Complex (IPRC) in Mahendragiri, validated the engine's ignition, steady-state performance, and thermal resilience under extreme operating conditions. ### Strategic Importance of NGLV The NGLV is envisioned as India's future workhorse rocket, combining heavy-lift capacity with cost-efficiency through reusable booster stages. Capable of carrying up to 30 tonnes to Low Earth Orbit (LEO), the rocket will play a foundational role in launching modules for the *Bharatiya Antariksha Station* (BAS) and undertaking ambitious crewed lunar missions. ### Technological Innovation Unlike traditional cryogenic engines that use liquid hydrogen, the semi-cryogenic engine uses refined kerosene (ISRO's specialized 'Isrokerox') combined with liquid oxygen. This combination provides higher payload density, easier storage, and significantly reduced operational costs, positioning India strongly in the global commercial launch market. ### Exam Relevance for Science & Tech Questions frequently test ISRO's launch vehicle hierarchy (SLV, ASLV, PSLV, GSLV, LVM3, and NGLV), propellant types, and upcoming deep space milestones in UPSC and state civil service exams.",
