    "keyHighlights": [
      "Conducted at the Aeronautical Test Range (ATR) in Chitradurga, Karnataka.",
      "The winged vehicle was released from an Indian Air Force Chinook helicopter at an altitude of 5 kilometers.",
      "Successfully performed autonomous crosswind landing maneuvers simulating orbital re-entry descent.",
      "Brings ISRO one step closer to developing a fully reusable two-stage-to-orbit (TSTO) launch vehicle."
    ],
    "fullContent": "The Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) achieved another milestone in aerospace engineering with the successful completion of the Reusable Launch Vehicle (RLV) LEX-3 mission. Tested at the Chitradurga Aeronautical Test Range, the winged prototype, 'Pushpak', demonstrated pinpoint autonomous landing capabilities after being dropped from a Chinook helicopter. The mission was specifically designed to test the vehicle's navigation algorithms, sensor fusion, and landing gear under simulated high-altitude crosswind conditions, which mirror actual orbital re-entry scenarios. Developing a reusable launch vehicle is a cornerstone of ISRO's long-term strategy to dramatically lower the cost of access to space. By recovering and reusing the most expensive components of a rocket—such as the booster stages and thermal protection systems—India can offer highly competitive commercial launch services globally. The data gathered from LEX-3 will be instrumental in finalizing the design for the orbital-class RLV prototype. For science and technology sections in competitive examinations, questions frequently focus on ISRO's key technological demonstrations. Candidates should be familiar with terms like TSTO (Two-Stage-to-Orbit), scramjet engines, and autonomous landing guidance systems, which represent the future trajectory of space exploration and satellite deployment.",
    "source": "GK Today",
    "sourceUrl": "",
    "publishedAt": "11 September 2026",
    "syncedAt": "2026-09-11T02:51:45.650Z"
