    "fullContent": "In a stellar achievement for India's aerospace capabilities, ISRO successfully executed a flight test of the advanced Hypersonic Technology Demonstration Vehicle (HSTDV) from the Satish Dhawan Space Centre in Sriharikota. The test vehicle achieved a sustained hypersonic velocity exceeding Mach 6, maintaining stable aerodynamic control and thermal protection at extreme atmospheric friction levels. The core of this success lies in the indigenous scramjet (supersonic combustion ramjet) engine, which utilizes atmospheric oxygen during flight, drastically reducing the weight of onboard oxidizers required for propulsion. The successful demonstration marks a monumental leap in India's aerospace engineering, paving the way for low-cost, highly efficient reusable launch vehicles (RLVs) for satellite deployment and deep space exploration. Additionally, the technology has immense strategic dual-use value, reinforcing India's defense capabilities in developing hypersonic cruise missiles that can bypass modern air defense radar systems. Science and technology segments in competitive examinations frequently test concepts related to propulsion systems. Aspirants must understand the fundamental difference between ramjets (subsonic combustion) and scramjets (supersonic combustion), as well as the significance of Mach numbers in aerospace classification.",
