    "fullContent": "The Union Cabinet, chaired by the Prime Minister, has given its green light for the comprehensive expansion of Phase II of the PM-KUSUM scheme. Originally launched to provide energy security to farmers along with increasing the share of renewable energy in the agriculture sector, the revised framework introduces augmented financial incentives and streamlined administrative processes. Under the expanded guidelines, the central financial assistance for installing standalone solar pumps has been increased from 30% to 40% of the benchmark cost in general states, and up to 60% in north-eastern and hilly terrains. Furthermore, Component C of the scheme, which deals with the solarization of grid-connected agricultural pumps, has been modified to allow farmer producer organizations (FPOs) to set up localized solar power plants. This will not only enable farmers to irrigate their fields reliably during daytime hours without depending on erratic grid supplies but also allow them to sell surplus power back to the distribution companies (discoms), creating an additional revenue stream. From a competitive exam perspective, aspirants preparing for UPSC, Banking, and State PSC examinations must note the core components of PM-KUSUM: Component A (setting up 10,000 MW of decentralized ground-mounted grid-connected renewable power plants), Component B (installation of 20 lakh standalone solar agriculture pumps), and Component C (solarization of 15 lakh grid-connected agriculture pumps). The scheme plays a pivotal role in India's broader commitment to achieving 500 GW of non-fossil fuel energy capacity and fulfilling its Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) under the Paris Agreement.",
