    "fullContent": "Marking a monumental milestone in diplomatic and economic relations, India and the European Union have formally concluded negotiations for the **Bilateral Investment Treaty (BIT)** in Brussels. The agreement comes after years of meticulous deliberations aimed at striking a delicate balance between attracting foreign direct investment (FDI) and safeguarding the regulatory autonomy of the Indian state. The newly finalized treaty introduces a balanced **Investor-State Dispute Settlement (ISDS)** mechanism, requiring foreign investors to exhaust local judicial remedies for a period of at least five years before initiating international arbitration. This safeguards domestic policy space, particularly in taxation, public health, and environmental conservation. Key sectors poised to benefit immensely from this pact include renewable energy, electric vehicle manufacturing, artificial intelligence, and pharmaceutical research. The treaty also institutionalizes a joint committee to address non-tariff barriers and streamline customs clearance procedures, significantly lowering the cost of doing business for European enterprises in India and vice versa. For aspirants preparing for **UPSC Civil Services and State PSC Mains (GS Paper II and III)**, this treaty serves as a prime case study in modern international trade diplomacy, multilateralism, and bilateral economic integration.",
