    "fullContent": "### ISRO Advances Cryogenic Capability with Upgraded CE-25 Engine The **Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO)** achieved a major technological milestone on 11 September 2026 by completing a long-duration hot test of its enhanced **CE-25 cryogenic engine**. The test was conducted at the ISRO Propulsion Complex (IPRC) in Mahendragiri, Tamil Nadu. #### Technical Improvements - **Increased Thrust**: The upgraded CE-25 engine operates at a thrust level of 22 tonnes (up from 20 tonnes), providing higher specific impulse for the upper stage (C25) of the LVM3 launch vehicle. - **Payload Boost**: The modification boosts LVM3’s payload capacity to Geostationary Transfer Orbit (GTO) to **4.5 metric tonnes**, enabling heavier communication satellites and deep-space modules to be launched from Sriharikota. - **Additive Manufacturing**: Key components of the turbopump and thrust chamber were manufactured using additive 3D-printing technologies developed by the **Liquid Propulsion Systems Centre (LPSC)**. #### Exam Relevance - **Engine**: CE-25 (Cryogenic Engine operating on LOX/LH2 - Liquid Oxygen / Liquid Hydrogen). - **Vehicle**: LVM3 (formerly GSLV Mk III). - **Location**: IPRC Mahendragiri, Tirunelveli district, Tamil Nadu.",
