    "fullContent": "### ISRO Milestone: Gaganyaan G2 Mission Success The **Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO)** achieved a major milestone in India's human spaceflight endeavor by completing the **Gaganyaan G2 uncrewed orbital mission**. The LVM3 heavy-lift launch vehicle lifted off from the Satish Dhawan Space Centre (SDSC SHAR) in Sriharikota, precisely placing the Crew Module (CM) and Service Module (SM) into a 400 km circular Earth orbit. #### Role of 'Vyommitra' Inside the pressurized Crew Module was **Vyommitra**, ISRO's indigenous female half-humanoid robot. During the multi-day mission, Vyommitra performed automated tasks, monitored cabin pressurization, read instrument panels, and communicated with mission control to simulate human physiological and operational conditions in space. #### Key Flight Objectives Achieved * Validation of the Environmental Control and Life Support System (ECLSS). * Testing of the Crew Escape System (CES) high-altitude separation protocols. * Successful re-entry sequence utilizing thermal shield protection systems during atmospheric entry at Mach 25 speeds. * Recovery of the module from the Bay of Bengal by the Indian Navy's Eastern Naval Command. #### Exam Takeaways For Science & Tech sections in competitive exams, aspirants should remember the specs of the **LVM3 rocket** (formerly GSLV Mk III), the distinction between crewed vs uncrewed test missions (TV-D1, G1, G2), the role of **Vyommitra**, and the target orbit of 400 km for India's upcoming crewed space mission.",
