    "fullContent": "### RBI Enhances Central Bank Digital Currency Infrastructure In a landmark regulatory update, the **Reserve Bank of India (RBI)** has expanded the operational capabilities of the **Digital Rupee (e₹-R)** by introducing offline functionality and integrating it with the **UPI Circle** framework. This development addresses critical accessibility bottlenecks, enabling transaction capabilities in remote or low-network regions across India. #### Key Features of the Upgraded e₹ Framework 1. **Offline Proximity Payments:** Uses Near Field Communication (NFC) and Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) technologies to execute peer-to-peer (P2P) and peer-to-merchant (P2M) transactions without an active internet connection. 2. **Delegated Payments via UPI Circle:** Allows primary account holders to grant partial transaction authority to trusted secondary users (e.g., family members or dependents) using the e₹ wallet. 3. **Programmable Subsidy Tokens:** Enables government agencies to disburse targeted benefit transfers that can only be spent at specified merchant categories (e.g., fertilizers, educational supplies). #### Regulatory & Economic Context The RBI launched the pilot for CBDC Retail (e₹-R) in December 2022. The introduction of offline capability and UPI interoperability is projected to push daily e₹ transaction volumes past **5 million**. For banking and economy exam preparations, candidates should understand the distinction between CBDC (sovereign digital liability) and commercial bank money, as well as the role of **NPCI** in driving interoperable payment rails under the Payment and Settlement Systems Act, 2007.",
