    "fullContent": "On 11 September 2026, the Defence Research and Development Organisation (DRDO), in collaboration with the Indian Air Force (IAF), successfully conducted the flight test of the Akash-NG (New Generation) Mark II air defense missile system from the Integrated Test Range (ITR) at Chandipur, off the coast of Odisha. The flight test successfully met all mission objectives, tracking a low-altitude, high-speed unmanned target aircraft simulating an incoming cruise missile. The Akash-NG Mark II features a dual-pulse solid rocket motor that significantly extends its operational interception range to 80 km, compared to the 30 km range of legacy Akash systems. It is equipped with an indigenous Ku-band Active Electronically Scanned Array (AESA) Radio Frequency (RF) seeker, enabling high precision during terminal guidance. Developed by DRDO's Defence Research and Development Laboratory (DRDL), Hyderabad, and manufactured by Bharat Dynamics Limited (BDL) with Bharat Electronics Limited (BEL) supplying the Multi-Function Radar (MFR), the system offers a 360-degree reaction capability with a system response time under 4 seconds. The system is designed for mobile deployment on high-mobility vehicles to protect tactical battle areas against saturation attacks. Key exam facts for defense aspirants: Akash is part of the original Integrated Guided Missile Development Programme (IGMDP) initiated in 1983; the NG variant utilizes a canisterized launcher system, enhancing shelf-life and enabling quick reaction capabilities.",
