    "fullContent": "The Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO) achieved a major technological milestone on 11 September 2026 by completing a full-duration 500-second qualification hot-test of its uprated CE-25 Cryogenic Engine. The test took place at the High Altitude Test Facility of the ISRO Propulsion Complex (IPRC) located in Mahendragiri, Tamil Nadu. The CE-25 engine powers the upper cryogenic stage (C25) of India's heaviest operational launch vehicle, the LVM3 (Launch Vehicle Mark-3). The upgraded variant incorporates 3D-printed thrust chambers and optimized liquid oxygen/liquid hydrogen propellant injectors. These modifications have raised the nominal thrust output from 20 tonnes (196 kN) to 22.5 tonnes (220 kN), boosting the rocket's payload delivery capability to Geostationary Transfer Orbit (GTO) from 4,000 kg to 4,450 kg. This enhanced lift capability is vital for supporting heavy module launches for the planned Bharatiya Antariksha Station (BAS-1 module targeted for 2028) and uncrewed test flights under the Gaganyaan human spaceflight programme. Additionally, higher payload capacity improves NewSpace India Limited's (NSIL) competitiveness in winning commercial launch contracts for heavy foreign communication satellites. Candidates preparing for Science & Tech sections in competitive exams should remember key details: LVM3 payload capabilities, the cryogenic combination used (Liquid Hydrogen as fuel, Liquid Oxygen as oxidizer), the role of IPRC Mahendragiri, and the distinction between Low Earth Orbit (LEO) and Geostationary Transfer Orbit (GTO).",
