    "fullContent": "The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) on 11 September 2026 issued a landmark regulatory circular titled 'Framework for Digital Settlement of Sovereign Green Securities.' This framework directly integrates Sovereign Green Bonds (SGBs) into the wholesale and retail segments of India's Central Bank Digital Currency (CBDC), commonly referred to as the Digital Rupee (e₹). Under the new architecture, programmable smart contracts built into the e₹ infrastructure will automatically execute interest payouts and redemption claims directly into holders' digital wallets, eliminating intermediary clearing delays and reducing operational overheads. To democratize participation in sustainable finance, the central bank reduced the minimum bidding threshold for retail investors buying green bonds via the RBI Retail Direct platform from ₹10,000 to ₹5,000. Furthermore, the framework enables non-resident Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPIs) operating out of GIFT City (Gujarat International Finance Tec-City) to execute real-time cross-border settlements using dedicated e₹-Wholesale channels. This operational shift is expected to enhance liquidity and lower the yields on green bonds, creating a favorable 'greenium' for sovereign debt issuances. For banking and economy exams (RBI Grade B, IBPS PO, UPSC Economy), candidates must understand the core concepts of Sovereign Green Bonds (first issued by India in FY 2022-23), the distinction between retail (e₹-R) and wholesale (e₹-W) CBDC, and the function of GIFT-IFSC as an offshore financial hub.",
    "source": "GK Today",
    "sourceUrl": "",
    "publishedAt": "11 September 2026",
    "syncedAt": "2026-09-11T02:18:47.785Z"
