    "fullContent": "On 11 September 2026, the Ministry of New and Renewable Energy (MNRE) officially announced the rollout of Phase-II of the flagship National Green Hydrogen Mission. Building upon the foundational regulatory frameworks and electrolyzer manufacturing incentives established during Phase-I (launched in January 2023), Phase-II shifts operational focus toward commercial deployment in hard-to-abate sectors. The primary focus of Phase-II is the decarbonization of heavy-duty mobility, including long-haul freight transportation, intercity bus corridors, and maritime shipping logistics. Under the updated framework, the government has set aside a dedicated sub-budget of ₹4,500 crore to fund capital expenditure subsidies for fleet operators integrating hydrogen fuel-cell vehicles (FCVs) and constructing green hydrogen refueling stations. Five major national corridors—including the Delhi-Mumbai Expressway and the Bengaluru-Chennai Industrial Corridor—have been designated for zero-emission freight transition. Furthermore, major ports like V.O. Chidambaranar Port in Tuticorin and Deendayal Port in Kandla are being developed as green hydrogen bunkering hubs to support international maritime compliance under International Maritime Organization (IMO) standards. For competitive examination candidates (UPSC, State PSCs), this initiative holds high relevance under General Studies Paper III (Economy and Environment). Key statistics to remember include India's overarching goal of producing 5 million metric tonnes (MMT) of green hydrogen per annum by 2030 and bringing down production costs below $1.50 per kilogram through indigenous electrolyzer manufacturing under the Strategic Interventions for Green Hydrogen Transition (SIGHT) scheme.",
