    "fullContent": "### Kameng South Notified as 34th Elephant Reserve In a landmark step for wildlife conservation, the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change (MoEFCC) has formally notified the **Kameng South Elephant Reserve** in **Arunachal Pradesh**. This declaration marks the establishment of **India’s 34th Elephant Reserve**, securing over 1,200 sq. km of contiguous forest cover vital for the Asian Elephant (*Elephas maximus*). #### Ecological Significance The newly designated reserve encompasses key forest divisions in West Kameng and East Kameng districts, serving as a vital migratory corridor between the **Pakke Tiger Reserve** in Arunachal Pradesh and the **Nameri National Park** in Assam. Preserving this continuous landscape prevents habitat fragmentation, ensuring genetic diversity among local elephant populations and reducing incidents of human-elephant conflict in fringe agricultural villages. #### Legal Framework & Project Elephant - **Project Elephant:** Launched by the Government of India in **1992** as a Centrally Sponsored Scheme to provide financial and technical support for wild elephant management. - **Protection Status:** The Asian Elephant is listed as **'Endangered'** on the IUCN Red List and protected under **Schedule I** of India’s Wildlife Protection Act, 1972. #### Competitive Exam Relevance - **New Reserve:** Kameng South Elephant Reserve (34th in India). - **State:** Arunachal Pradesh. - **Launch Year of Project Elephant:** 1992. - **IUCN Status of Asian Elephant:** Endangered.",
