    "fullContent": "### Successful Test of Astra Mark-III Missile India’s defence technology capabilities achieved another milestone as the **Defence Research and Development Organisation (DRDO)** successfully flight-tested the **Astra Mark-III** Beyond-Visual-Range Air-to-Air Missile (BVRAAM) from an integrated test range off the coast of Chandipur, Odisha. The missile was launched from a modified Sukhoi Su-30MKI fighter platform and successfully intercepted a high-speed unmanned aerial target at extreme distance. #### Technological Advancements: SFDR Propulsion The defining feature of Astra Mark-III is its **Solid Fuel Ducted Ramjet (SFDR)** engine system. Unlike traditional solid-rocket motors that carry both fuel and oxidizer, an SFDR engine draws oxygen directly from the surrounding atmosphere during supersonic flight. This significantly saves weight and allows the missile to maintain continuous acceleration during its terminal phase, ensuring a minimal 'no-escape zone' for target aircraft. #### Strategic Capabilities - **Range:** 340+ km, positioning India among an elite group of nations (USA, Russia, France) possessing long-range air-to-air ramjet technology. - **Platform Compatibility:** Custom-designed to equip Su-30MKI, Rafale, and the indigenous Tejas Mk1A / Mk2 combat aircraft. - **Guidance System:** Uses an indigenous dual-mode active radar seeker integrated with fiber-optic gyroscopes for mid-course updates. #### Competitive Exam Relevance - **Developer:** DRDO in collaboration with Defence Metallurgy Research Laboratory (DMRL). - **Core Technology:** SFDR (Solid Fuel Ducted Ramjet). - **Category:** Beyond-Visual-Range Air-to-Air Missile (BVRAAM).",
    "source": "GK Today",
