    "fullContent": "### 18th East Asia Summit Working Group Meeting The Ministry of External Affairs (MEA), India, hosted senior diplomats and strategic experts from 18 member nations in New Delhi for the **18th East Asia Summit (EAS) Senior Officials' Working Group Meeting**. The high-level conference focused on addressing emerging security vulnerabilities in the Indo-Pacific region, ensuring uninterrupted sea lanes of communication, and promoting collaborative climate technology. #### Key Discussions and Outcomes The summit reaffirmed the centrality of ASEAN in the Indo-Pacific construct. A landmark outcome was the formal drafting of the **New Delhi Action Framework for Maritime Cooperation**, which targets maritime domain awareness, search-and-rescue interoperability, and illegal, unreported, and unregulated (IUU) fishing prevention. Delegates also finalized plans for a shared framework on microgrid technology transfer to support island nations in the Pacific. #### About the East Asia Summit (EAS) Established in 2005, the East Asia Summit is a premier premier leaders-led forum for dealing with strategic, political, and economic challenges in the Indo-Pacific. It comprises **18 countries**: the 10 ASEAN member states (Brunei, Cambodia, Indonesia, Laos, Malaysia, Myanmar, Philippines, Singapore, Thailand, Vietnam) along with eight dialogue partners: Australia, China, India, Japan, New Zealand, Republic of Korea, Russia, and the United States. #### Competitive Exam Relevance - **Formation Year:** 2005 (First Summit held in Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia). - **Member Nations:** 10 ASEAN + 8 Dialogue Partners. - **Topic Importance:** International Relations, Indo-Pacific diplomacy, UNCLOS, ASEAN-India Strategic Partnership.",
