    "fullContent": "### PM-PRANAM Phase II Expansion In a major push toward sustainable agriculture, the Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs (CCEA) chaired by the Prime Minister has approved the second phase of the **PM-PRANAM** (*Programme for Restoration, Awareness, Nourishment and Amelioration of Mother Earth*) scheme. The revamped framework introduces a structured financial mechanism to assist state governments in curtailing reliance on chemical fertilizers like urea and DAP. #### Key Objectives and Financial Architecture Under Phase II, the central government will allocate **50% of the total fertilizer subsidy saved** by a state directly back to that state as an incentive grant. Out of this grant, 70% must be utilized for asset creation aimed at the adoption of alternative fertilizers, such as setting up compressed biogas (CBG) plants, bio-fertilizer manufacturing units, and village-level composting centers. The remaining 30% can be used to reward local Panchayats and farmers showcasing exemplary work in reducing chemical inputs. #### Background and Strategic Need India’s annual fertilizer subsidy bill has faced upward pressure due to volatile global raw material prices. The PM-PRANAM scheme was originally introduced to balance soil health and optimize fiscal expenditure. Over-reliance on synthetic nitrogenous fertilizers has led to land degradation, reduced water table quality, and altered natural soil pH in major agricultural belts. #### Competitive Exam Relevance - **Scheme Name:** PM-PRANAM (Ministry of Chemicals and Fertilizers). - **Target:** Balanced application of NPK (Nitrogen, Phosphorus, Potassium) and promotion of nano-urea/bio-fertilizers. - **Exam Focus:** Sustainable agriculture, environmental conservation, fiscal deficit management, and state incentive structures.",
    "source": "GK Today",
