    "fullContent": "### Space Tech Breakthrough The **Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO)** successfully completed a long-duration hot test of its **SCE-200 Semi-Cryogenic Engine** at the High-Altitude Test Facility at ISRO Propulsion Complex (IPRC) in Mahendragiri, Tamil Nadu. The engine fired as planned for **150 seconds**, meeting all target parameters of combustion stability and thrust performance. ### Technical Specifications & Advantages * **Thrust Capacity:** Produces 2,000 kiloNewtons (kN) of nominal thrust in sea-level conditions. * **Propellant Combination:** Employs **Isrosene** (a specialized aviation-grade refined kerosene) as fuel and **Liquid Oxygen (LOX)** as oxidizer. * **Safety & Efficiency:** Unlike full cryogenic engines requiring liquid hydrogen (stored at -253°C), semi-cryogenic engines use liquid kerosene stored at ambient temperatures, making fuel handling vastly safer and more cost-efficient. ### Strategic Importance The SCE-200 will serve as the core booster stage for India's upcoming **Next-Gen Launch Vehicles (NGLV)** and upgraded variants of LVM3. It boosts India's Geostationary Transfer Orbit (GTO) payload capacity from 4 tonnes to **over 6 tonnes**, reducing dependency on foreign commercial launchers for heavy communication satellites. ### Exam Relevance * **UPSC/SSC Science & Tech:** Difference between Cryogenic (LOX + LH2) and Semi-Cryogenic (LOX + Kerosene) engines. * **Key Facilities:** IPRC Mahendragiri, Liquid Propulsion Systems Centre (LPSC).",
